  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Server**\n\nOkay, I\'m now structuring the `server.ts` file. My focus shifted to designing the server-side Gemini integration within it. Requirements are clear: port 3000, bind to \"0.0.0.0\", and mount Vite. The overall goal is becoming clearer, and the direction for the `server.ts` file design is now focused.\n\n\n**Developing API Route**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the API route for the \"Surprise Me\" action. I\'m thinking of `/api/curate-artwork` or `/api/gemini/surprise`. I\'m going to make sure the route can receive a `mood`, `theme`, or `vibe` from the client, or maybe generate it itself. It should call the `@google/genai` SDK with the `gemini-3.5-flash` model. I\'ve re-reviewed the server-side initialization using `GoogleGenAI` and considered how to handle a missing `GEMINI_API_KEY`.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve just implemented a robust error-handling strategy for the API key. I created a lazy initialization function, `getGeminiClient()`, that checks for the API key before attempting to initialize the Gemini client. If the key is missing, it logs a warning and returns null. This prevents server crashes and gracefully falls back to an offline curation mode, ensuring the app functions even without a key. This safeguard ensures that we have a fallback for pre-curated pieces.\n\n\n**Defining the Endpoint Response**\n\nI\'m now concretely defining the `/api/surprise` endpoint\'s output. I\'ve landed on a structured JSON response including title, artist, description, price, category, and style. I\'m focusing on \"visualType,\" and considering both real-time CSS/SVG generation, and pre-seeded artwork, or even a hybrid approach. I\'m leaning toward in-browser generation for stunning visuals.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Output**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to the in-browser generative approach for visuals. I\'m focusing on dynamic digital compositions within the 3D frame, rendered using CSS patterns, canvas grids, or gradients. This makes the system visually bulletproof! The AI-curated piece will be rendered inside the frame as styled canvas elements. I\'m thinking of generating a `primaryColor`, `secondaryColor`, `tertiaryColor` and an `artStyle` to achieve this. Then, I can use those values within the React code to render a `<canvas>` element or modern CSS layouts.\n\n\n**Refining the Response**\n\nI\'m now instructing the Gemini model to output a clean JSON response with specific fields: title, description, primary color, secondary color, vibe, price, size, and medium. I\'m also planning offline examples as a fallback. Moreover, I\'m now drafting `/server.ts` to correctly handle imports, especially ensuring the use of ES import statements for `express`, `path`, and `vite`. I\'m verifying the correct use of `import` statements, and preparing for static frontend serving in production.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now create the server entry point `server.ts`. This file implements our Express backend server, handles the server-side Gemini curation API for the \"\342\234\250 SURPRISE ME!\" feature, and seamlessly integrates the Vite middleware for local development and product build hosting on Port 3000."
    thinking_duration {
