  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Integrating App.tsx**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating all the components, specifically within `App.tsx`. My current thought process involves designing the overall flow within `App.tsx`, and I\'m starting by reviewing the existing structure to understand its foundation. This initial analysis should inform how to best combine all the parts into a cohesive whole.\n\n\n**Defining App.tsx Structure**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a clearer vision for the `App.tsx` structure. My main goal is to create a fluid mobile container with a `#FAF9F6` background. For desktop users, it\'ll sit within a beautiful mobile phone frame, complete with glass reflections. The mobile frame disappears on smaller screens, allowing the content to fill the screen. A subtle artistic background overlay will complement the gallery\'s theme.\n\n\n**Structuring Menu Options**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the menu structure. The objective is a clean aesthetic, so I\'m envisioning three perfectly square buttons stacked vertically. Each button will have a pristine white interior framed by a distinct hand-drawn purple brushstroke border, achieved using the `<HandDrawnBorder>` component. I am already moving to implement the visual design.\n\n\n**Refining Menu Interaction**\n\nI\'m now zeroed in on refining the button interaction and sub-menu display. I want to add clean display typography with either Inter or Space Grotesk. Hover effects will include elegant hand-drawn wiggles, or high-contrast styling. I need the vertical stack to be perfect, even on smaller screens, using vertical offsets when needed. I need to handle sub-menu display using `menuState`: `\"main\" | \"paintings-submenu\" | \"photography-submenu\" | \"artwork-list\" | \"artwork-detail\"`. I\'ll create a smooth navigation controller by setting `menuState` based on user taps. From sub-menus, the square buttons will maintain the hand-drawn border.\n\n\n**Implementing Artwork Display**\n\nI\'m now focusing on navigation between artwork displays. Tapping sub-category buttons triggers the artwork grid display with its respective style. Clicking an artwork card takes the `menuState` to the detail view, where I\'ll implement the 3D floating box frame and \"Add to Basket\". I need to include a back arrow for smooth navigation.\n\n**Designing Footers**\n\nI am designing the footer. I plan to add small, tappable icons for user story and Instagram on the left, and a shopping basket on the right. The user story icon will be a gradient-bordered circle.\n\n\n**Designing Footer Features**\n\nI\'m now implementing the footer features. My thoughts focus on launching a user story with an intuitive design, which perfectly fits the icon! I plan to use a Lucide `Instagram` icon, linking it to a pop-up alert or a social feed mockup, all styled with the hand-drawn theme. A Lucide `ShoppingBasket` icon with a badge will trigger our overlay drawer. The result will be a beautiful bottom tab-bar.\n\n\n**Implementing Curation Flow**\n\nMy focus is now on the `/curate-artwork` endpoint. I\'m excited about the spinning cursor, with its AI-Studio-themed progress notes. Once it completes, I\'m transitioning directly to the 3D Canvas Mockup detail screen. I\'m also confirming we can handle multiple basket items, each with custom frame selections, stored as a `basket` array of `BasketItem` objects.\n\n\n**Implementing App.tsx Code**\n\nI\'m now diving into the code for `src/App.tsx`, and planning the overall structure. My current focus involves establishing the core state management for the application. I\'ll be using `lucide-react` icons for consistent and clean design. I want to make the app incredibly smooth and visually appealing. The goal is to make it easy to scroll and a breeze for users to navigate.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now write the full implementation of `/src/App.tsx` using `create_file` to replace the empty template. It establishes the navigation controller, mobile-phone device emulator frame, the three hand-drawn brushstroke-bordered purple squares, sub-menus, artwork detail drawers, and interactive basket/story modal integrations."
